        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "refactor(skills): normalize ClawHub search limits and cleanup legacy constants",
        "body": "## Summary\n\n- **Type**: Refactor (defensive programming + code cleanup)\n- **Motivation**: Normalize ClawHub skill-search limits at the lifecycle layer for consistency with plugin search, and remove unused legacy constants.\n- **What changed**: \n  - `src/skills/lifecycle/clawhub.ts` — Added `resolveSkillSearchLimit` helper that truncates fractional values and validates positivity\n  - `src/infra/clawhub.test.ts` — Added transport-layer tests verifying verbatim limit forwarding\n  - `src/skills/lifecycle/clawhub.test.ts` — Added caller-layer tests for normalization behavior\n  - Removed unused `DEFAULT_SKILL_SEARCH_LIMIT` constant\n- **What did NOT change**: \n  - Gateway schema maximum (still 1-100)\n  - Native CLI parser (still accepts any positive integer)\n  - ClawHub service behavior (accepts limits above 100)\n\n## Architecture Context\n\n**Current validation chain:**\n1. **CLI entry**: `parseStrictPositiveIntOption` accepts only positive integers (`/^[+-]?\\d+$/`)\n2. **Gateway entry**: Schema enforces `limit: Type.Integer({ minimum: 1, maximum: 100 })`\n3. **Lifecycle layer** (this PR): Defensive normalization for consistency\n\n**Why this change:**\n- **Code consistency**: Plugin search (`catalog-search.ts`) has similar normalization; skill search should match\n- **Defensive programming**: While current CLI/Gateway callers validate inputs, lifecycle normalization provides an additional safety layer\n- **Code cleanup**: Removed unused `DEFAULT_SKILL_SEARCH_LIMIT` constant\n\n**Acknowledgment**: No current broken path exists. CLI and Gateway already validate inputs. This is a defensive improvement, not a bug fix.\n\n## Value\n\n- ✅ **Code consistency**: Aligns with plugin search normalization pattern\n- ✅ **Code cleanup**: Removes unused legacy constant\n- ✅ **Future-proofing**: Defensive layer for potential future callers\n- ✅ **Test coverage**: Added transport-layer tests for verbatim forwarding\n\n## Real behavior proof\n\n- **Environment**: Vitest unit tests\n- **Command**: `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/skills/lifecycle/clawhub.test.ts --run`\n- **Evidence**:\n  ```text\n  searchSkillsFromClawHub({ limit: 999_999 })\n    → calls searchClawHubSkills({ limit: 999_999 })  (no cap) ✅\n\n  searchSkillsFromClawHub({ limit: 20 })\n    → calls searchClawHubSkills({ limit: 20 })  (unchanged) ✅\n\n  searchSkillsFromClawHub({})\n    → calls searchClawHubSkills({ limit: undefined })  (unchanged) ✅\n\n  searchSkillsFromClawHub({ limit: 0.5 })\n    → calls searchClawHubSkills({ limit: undefined })  (truncated to 0, omitted) ✅\n\n  searchSkillsFromClawHub({ limit: 1.5 })\n    → calls searchClawHubSkills({ limit: 1 })  (truncated, valid) ✅\n  ```\n\n## Regression Test Plan\n\n- Coverage: Unit tests\n- Target files: `src/skills/lifecycle/clawhub.test.ts`, `src/infra/clawhub.test.ts`\n- Scenarios:\n  - Large limit forwarded without cap (999,999)\n  - Normal limit forwarded unchanged (20)\n  - No limit remains undefined\n  - Fractional limit below 1 omitted after truncation (0.5 → 0 → undefined)\n  - Fractional limit above 1 truncated and forwarded (1.5 → 1)\n\n---\n\n@clawsweeper re-review\n",
